❯ given the list of newly created users in lldap how do i get all the password? 

  Thought for 5s (ctrl+o to expand)

● I'll investigate how users are created in LLDAP in this project, since the
  answer depends on how the passwords were set when the users were created.

  Thought for 17s, searched for 1 pattern, read 2 files, listed 2 directories (ctrl+o to expand)

● You can't recover plaintext passwords from LLDAP itself — it only stores
  salted argon2id hashes. But in this project, every user is created by a script
  that persists the plaintext password to a file at creation time. That's where
  you read them from.

  Where the passwords live

  There are two sets of LLDAP users, and each writes its passwords to a
  different file:

  1. Provisioning pool users (user01…user30) — created by
  test_script/scripts/create_users.sh
  /home/ubuntu/libcloud_nutanix/test_script/scripts/generated/users.env   (mode
  0600)
  Vars look like LIBCLOUD_USER_01_PASSWORD=… … LIBCLOUD_USER_30_PASSWORD.

  2. Superadmin + per-cloud owners/admins/viewers — created by setup.sh →
  lldap_ensure_user.sh
  /home/ubuntu/libcloud_nutanix/dex/generated/dex.env
  /home/ubuntu/libcloud_nutanix/test_script/generated/dex.env   (a copy)
  Vars: LIBCLOUD_SUPERADMIN_PASSWORD, LIBCLOUD_PASSWORD_AWS_OWNER,
  LIBCLOUD_PASSWORD_AWS_ADMIN, LIBCLOUD_PASSWORD_AWS_VIEWER,
  LIBCLOUD_PASSWORD_NTNX_OWNER, LIBCLOUD_PASSWORD_NTNX_ADMIN,
  LIBCLOUD_PASSWORD_NTNX_VIEWER, LIBCLOUD_PASSWORD_CLOUD_DENIED.

  How to pull them all

  # Pool users (user01..user30)
  grep 'LIBCLOUD_USER_[0-9]*_PASSWORD=' test_script/scripts/generated/users.env

  # Superadmin + cloud users
  grep -E 'LIBCLOUD_(SUPERADMIN|PASSWORD_.*)_PASSWORD=' dex/generated/dex.env

  Or just view the whole files (they're the only plaintext source):
  cat test_script/scripts/generated/users.env
  cat dex/generated/dex.env

  Important caveats

  - Re-runs reuse, never rotate — create_users.sh sources users.env first so it
    won't change a password on a re-run (LLDAP's ensure_user resets the password
    when the user already exists, so the file and LLDAP stay in sync only if
    you don't delete the file).
  - LLDAP holds hashes only. If someone created a user by hand in the LLDAP UI
    (or create-user.sh was run standalone and printed a password to stdout that
    wasn't captured), there is no stored plaintext to recover — you'd have to
    reset the password via lldap/scripts/set-password.py.
  - Vault app-role tokens (not passwords, but often what you actually want next)
    are in test_script/scripts/generated/tokens/*.json (superadmin.json,
    aws1-owner.json, aws2-owner.json).

  Want me to dump the actual list of usernames with their passwords into a
  single users:password table for you?

